      "created_at": "2016-12-17T10:51:06Z",
      "updated_at": "2016-12-17T10:51:06Z",
      "body": "@pmlopes I'd disagree for 2 reasons.\r\n\r\n1) username and password are 2 things that MongoAuth tracks as special fields (MongoAuth knows which key they are stored under). So why merge username and password with the template, but leave out permissions and roles? Also, this would require the caller to also know the keys under which username and password are stored.\r\n\r\n2) the password shouldn't be stored in the JSON. MongoAuth takes care of the hashing strategy and only stores the hash. Sure, it could replace the password out of the template and replace it with the hash, but that seems more error prone.\r\n\r\nI'd suggest something like\r\n\r\n```\r\nvoid insertUser(JsonObject template, String username, String password, List<String> roles, List<String> permissions,  Handler<AsyncResult<String>> resultHandler) {\r\n  //Throw error if username or password field are already specified in template\r\n  //Throw error if roles/permissions are not null and are already specified in template\r\n  //Functionality as it exists now.\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nI'm willing to make a PR if you agree with this approach and if you let me know what to do with the existing method? (Adjust it, remove it, leave it be)",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "pin": null,
